A42. What people do to make a clean-up in hard times. ~ BATHE
A44. The way the poet's last summer's hat looks. ~ OLDEN
A46. A good thing to do on a reducing scale. ~ WEIGH
A48. Easy money from Brazil. ~ REI
A50. Just one thing after another. ~ TIER
A51. You will like this one—it's great. ~ LARGE
A52. A very unladylike person. (And so's your old man.) (abbr.) ~ MR
A53. A questionable word. ~ HOW
A54. A descendant of one of the early Americans. ~ PAPOOSE
A56. Where bossy has lunch every day. ~ LEA
A57. Continues in existence (even after a second cut in salary). ~ IS
A58. This is just a fare car. ~ BUS
A59. Better keep on the right side of this one if you want to avoid trouble. (abbr.) ~ ST
A60. Up on one's toes. ~ ALERT
A62. They do this on a frequency of two motorcycles. ~ PURSUE
A64. You may find a lot of strings attached to this. ~ FRINGE
A65. This was very confectionate, when granddad went wooing. ~ GUMDROP
A66. These movements are on foot in some of the party circles. ~ WALTZES

D1. These kind of people may appear small but this one has never been known to be close. ~ DISTANT
D2. 100% ~ ENTIRE
D3. One half of two. ~ TWIN
D4. This one and its mate are always having a row. ~ OAR
D5. This bunch of stars always stick together out in Hollywood. (Their show is a bear.) ~ URSA
D6. This is not as citified as Hor. 59. (abbr.) ~ RD
D7. Isn't science wonderful? Why with this it can literally make a conscience. ~ CON
D8. Unlike the man in the song this one left her flat when her hair turned to silver. ~ YOUTH
D9. This is what Mark used to call her. ~ CLEO
D10. This is now allowed in all puzzles. ~ LET
D11. A choice word. ~ OR
D12. One thing a Scotchman's wife can use lavishly. ~ ECONOMY
D14. Right in the face of the enemy this stuck out to the bitter end. ~ CIGAR
D16. A little sulky. (Archaic) ~ MOODY
D17. You will always find this one sober. ~ SOLEMN
D20. Don't slip up on this one. ~ GREASE
D22. This is a bad one to get stuck on. ~ TAR
D25. A beach cape. ~ COD
D26. The outcome of the egg business. ~ HATCH
D28. The big fight of 1917. ~ WAR
D29. A good thing to do after being all ruffled up. ~ PREEN
D30. Even a Scotchman couldn't raise much on this. ~ HEATH
D32. What they are apt to do to the umpire. ~ REFER
D34. Reflections of — screen star. ~ IMAGES
D36. You will find these in the book section. ~ FOLIOS
D38. This one is short one V. ~ OER
D39. What she does to keep her hands so beautiful. ~ NOTHING
D41. What professional boxers do with their wise cracks. ~ SWAP
D42. The fellow with a one-track mind. ~ BIGOT
D43. What Pittsburgh has contributed to the sporting world. ~ PIRATES
D45. This gets the drop on things by getting there early in the morning. ~ DEW
D47. This is very irregular. ~ EROSE
D49. "Come out of it." ~ EMERGE
D51. What Will Rogers makes use of when he throws a party. ~ LASSO
D54. The way cats broadcast their ideas on pacifism. ~ PURR
D55. After a last payment on a count, she bought this one cheap from a title and trust company. ~ EARL
D56. This man usually beats the game but his outlook is forbidding. ~ LENZ
D58. "Yes, sir, she's my baby now," sings Mother Nature in the spring. ~ BUD
D61. What the whole caravan of camels did when they dropped on the pavement. ~ LIT
D62. You can have the whole afternoon and evening for this. ~ PM
D63. This can easily be made on high. ~ UP
D64. Just "sing something simple." ~ FA


